Add a sbi_platform_final_exit hook that writes a "PARKED__" magic at
a known DRAM offset before the warmboot loop spins, plus a stub
sbi_system_reset_device so SBI SRST is reported supported and falls
through to sbi_exit instead of the kernel halting locally, plus a
custom IPI event ("bhx_force_park") for host-driven force-reboot
of running guests.

Phases 1+2+4a+5 of bhx#166. Phase 1 was the smallest possible
verification that the SBI SRST fall-through path lands in our hook.
Phase 2 added multi-hart convergence: the SRST-issuing hart polls
peer HSM state until every peer reaches SBI_HSM_STATE_STOPPED before
announcing PARKED. Phase 4a publishes hart 0's release metadata so
the host can drive a release-from-purgatory without re-deriving any
of the addresses. Phase 5 adds the bhx_force_park IPI event +
metadata: the host writes a magic to scratch->ipi_type and fires
CLINT MSIP[0] to deliver an M-mode software interrupt; OpenSBI's
existing IPI dispatcher routes to our process callback, which calls
sbi_system_reset (same path as a guest-issued SBI SRST). Recovers
any kernel-level wedge — Linux S-mode `sstatus.SIE=0` cannot mask
M-mode interrupts.

Why a stub reset device is needed (Phase 1): without ANY
system_reset_device registered, sbi_system_reset_supported() returns
false, so the guest kernel's reboot/halt path sees the SBI SRST
extension as unsupported and just halts in place — never invoking
SBI SRST, so our final_exit hook never fires.

Status block layout (all u64 little-endian; status_word written LAST
as a publish barrier so the host trusts the rest of the block once
it sees the PARKED magic):
  +0x00 : status word (PARKED magic / 0)
  +0x08 : peers convergence bitmask
  +0x10 : hart 0's `&scratch->next_addr` PA   (Phase 4a; SRST-time)
  +0x18 : hart 0's `&scratch->next_mode` PA   (Phase 4a; SRST-time)
  +0x20 : hart 0's `&scratch->next_arg1` PA   (Phase 4a; SRST-time)
  +0x28 : hart 0's HSM state PA               (Phase 4a; SRST-time)
  +0x30 : CLINT MSIP[0] PA                    (Phase 4a + 5; published
                                                 at cold init too)
  +0x38 : force-park request PA               (Phase 5; cold-init)
  +0x40 : force-park request value            (Phase 5; cold-init)

Status offset 0xE0000 lives in the firmware's reserved range past
OpenSBI's .text/.data/.bss + scratch (typically <300 KiB) and before
the kernel-DTB at +0x100000.

Phase 5 force-park sequence (host side):
  1. Read FORCE_PARK_REQ_PA + FORCE_PARK_REQ_VALUE + MSIP_PA from the
     status block (published at cold init, valid for any slot state).
  2. Write the value to the request PA. Sets the bhx_force_park
     event-pending bit in hart 0's `sbi_ipi_data->ipi_type`. No
     concurrent IPI sender (Linux can't issue M-mode IPIs), so a
     direct u64 write is equivalent to atomic-OR.
  3. Write 1 to MSIP_PA — delivers the M-mode software interrupt.
  4. Hart 0 traps; OpenSBI's IPI dispatcher walks ipi_type and calls
     bhx_force_park_process, which calls sbi_system_reset(SHUTDOWN, 0)
     — same path as a guest-issued SBI SRST. Peers get the halt-IPI,
     everyone walks final_exit + warmboot, host sees PARKED magic.
  5. Host runs the standard release-from-purgatory.

Two new helpers in OpenSBI lib/sbi/ expose otherwise-file-static
state to the patched generic platform:

  - sbi_hsm_hart_state_addr(hartindex) — PA of a hart's HSM state
    atomic_t (Phase 4a).
  - sbi_ipi_data_addr(hartindex) — PA of a hart's ipi_type bitmap
    (Phase 5).

State quiesce on the SRST-issuing hart (clearing pending CLINT MSIP /
mtimecmp / external IRQs) is already done by sbi_exit's earlier
calls to sbi_timer_exit / sbi_ipi_exit / sbi_irqchip_exit; we don't
duplicate that here. Cache flush + PMP re-validation are deferred
to the host's release path (see #166 Phase 4b).

